<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- TEMPLATE. install.sh renders every double-brace placeholder for the machine it runs on and
	     installs the result; the template itself is never installed and never hand-edited
	     for one machine. Placeholders: REPO_DIR (this checkout), CLAUDE_BIN and CONFIG_DIR
	     (stack.config runner.claude_bin / runner.config_dir, overridable with
	     LOOP_INSTALL_CLAUDE_BIN / LOOP_INSTALL_CONFIG_DIR), HOME (the installing user's), and
	     PATH (derived from where those binaries resolve, overridable with LOOP_INSTALL_PATH).
	     Everything that is NOT a placeholder is a decision and is tracked here: the schedule,
	     the arguments, the priority class. See JOB-CONTRACT.md for what the runner promises.
	     The vcs_host the sweep talks to is NOT in this file: pr_feed.py reads it from
	     stack.config adapters.vcs_host, and the target checkout from LOOP_APP_ROOT or
	     app.repo_dir. Add a LOOP_APP_ROOT key to EnvironmentVariables only to override that.
	     NOTE for the next editor: keep double hyphens out of every comment in this file. XML
	     forbids them inside a comment; plutil accepts them anyway, but the python plistlib
	     pass in install.sh does not, and it is the one that gates arming. Spell flags without
	     their leading dashes in prose. -->
	<key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
	<dict>
		<key>LOOP_CLAUDE_BIN</key>
		<string>{{CLAUDE_BIN}}</string>
		<key>CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR</key>
		<string>{{CONFIG_DIR}}</string>
		<key>HOME</key>
		<string>{{HOME}}</string>
		<key>LANG</key>
		<string>en_US.UTF-8</string>
		<key>PATH</key>
		<string>{{PATH}}</string>
	</dict>
	<key>Label</key>
	<string>com.loops.pr-review</string>
	<key>LowPriorityIO</key>
	<true/>
	<key>Nice</key>
	<integer>5</integer>
	<key>ProcessType</key>
	<string>Background</string>
	<!-- ARGUMENTS. /bin/bash is index 0 and the runner index 1 (install.sh reads index 1 to
	     find the script it preflights); everything after is a flag or a flag's value, and a
	     flag and its value are ALWAYS two separate string elements.

	     timeout 2400: 40 minutes of wall clock for the ONE model call. Bounded above by
	     pr_feed's tick lock, whose TTL is 2700s: a tick that outlives its own lock lets the
	     next one see "stale" and start sweeping while this one is still posting. This number
	     and pr_review_loop.prs_per_tick in loop-limits.json are ONE decision: budget roughly
	     900s of model call per pull request, so two fit here with headroom and finish inside
	     the lock TTL, and three (3 x 900 = 2700) do not fit at all. prs_per_tick therefore
	     lives in loop-limits.json ONLY. A limit flag here would OVERRIDE the tracked decision
	     (install.sh's preflight says so out loud), and two copies of one number are how two
	     numbers come to disagree. If you change this timeout, re-derive prs_per_tick from the
	     per-PR figure, and vice versa.

	     THE TEMPLATE SHIPS DRY-RUN AND SCOPE OWN. Two flags are deliberately ABSENT:
	       live          turns posting on. Without it a tick does the full review and writes a
	                     byte-faithful preview plus the tick report; nothing reaches the vcs
	                     host. Comment threads posted live carry the identity the vcs_host
	                     adapter resolves for the runner, so on a colleague's pull request they
	                     appear to come from that person. Add this element only after the gate
	                     in FIRST-RUN-pr-review.md, and set loop-limits.json posting_default
	                     to live in the same edit so the record matches the job.
	       scope all     reviews EVERY active non-draft PR targeting the base branch,
	                     colleagues' included, not just the runner identity's own. It has to be
	                     HERE and not only in loop-limits.json: pr_review.sh REFUSES a live tick
	                     (exit 25) whose widened scope was inherited from the JSON alone, so the
	                     file's value and this pair are both required. Reverting means removing
	                     both halves.
	     install.sh's preflight prints the effective posture (dry-run or live, and the
	     effective scope) so an absence is never invisible at the arming prompt. -->
	<key>ProgramArguments</key>
	<array>
		<string>/bin/bash</string>
		<string>{{REPO_DIR}}/.claude/skills/dev-loop-pr-review/scripts/pr_review.sh</string>
		<string>--timeout</string>
		<string>2400</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Arming is never a run. -->
	<key>RunAtLoad</key>
	<false/>
	<key>StandardErrorPath</key>
	<string>{{REPO_DIR}}/.claude/loops/pr-review.launchd.err.log</string>
	<key>StandardOutPath</key>
	<string>{{REPO_DIR}}/.claude/loops/pr-review.launchd.out.log</string>
	<!-- HOURLY. Minute is set and Hour is deliberately ABSENT: launchd treats an omitted
	     field as a WILDCARD, not as zero, so this fires at the given minute of every hour,
	     24x/day, wall-clock aligned. One entry rather than 24, so a future edit cannot desync
	     them. Do NOT "fix" this by adding an Hour key: `Hour 0` is not a no-op here, it would
	     narrow the job from 24 runs a day to one at midnight.
	     Once a day this slot coincides with com.loops.qa-dream (read that template for its
	     slot; do not restate it here). The two are SERIALIZED by the shared cross-agent lock
	     (lib/agent_lock.sh): whichever job gets the bolt runs, the other logs
	     `event=skipped reason=agent_lock_held` naming the holder and exits 0. On that slot it
	     is normally the dream winning and this tick coming back an hour later, which costs
	     nothing: the PR queue is durable and keyed on PR id plus head commit. WHY A LOCK AND
	     NOT A DIFFERENT MINUTE: launchd COALESCES missed calendar intervals into one event on
	     wake, so a laptop asleep through both slots fires both jobs at the same instant
	     whatever their minutes say. A lock is the only option that survives a wake.
	     Both runners classify a model-API 429 and exit 29 (out of window, not a fault), so a
	     starved job reports itself honestly instead of looking broken. -->
	<key>StartCalendarInterval</key>
	<dict>
		<key>Minute</key>
		<integer>0</integer>
	</dict>
	<key>WorkingDirectory</key>
	<string>{{REPO_DIR}}</string>
</dict>
</plist>
